.page rm 1 remove files or directories

# SYNOPSIS
*rm* [*-rRfv*] [*--*] _FILE_...

# DESCRIPTION
`rm` deletes each _FILE_ for good: nothing is kept. If you may want it back, send it to the Netherworld with trash(1) instead. Without `-r` a directory is refused with `Is a directory`.

# OPTIONS
@ *-r*, *-R*
    Remove directories and everything below them.
@ *-f*
    Force: ignore files that do not exist, never complain, and do not insist on a file operand.
@ *-v*
    Say what is removed.
@ *-i*
    Accepted and ignored: `rm` never asks.
@ *--*
    End of options, for files whose names start with a dash.

# EXIT STATUS
0, 1 when something could not be removed or the operand is missing, 2 for an unknown option.

# EXAMPLES
    rm old.txt
    rm -rf /tmp/build
    rm -- -strange-name

# SEE ALSO
trash(1), rmdir(1), find(1)
